          %h2#history History
          %p
            In his monograph on (co-)extensions of monoids, Leech introduced a construction that had also been used for other purposes by MacLane, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+arrow+category"} twisted arrow category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baues"} Baues
            and Wirshing introduced what is now usually called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baues-Wirsching+cohomology"} Baues-Wirsching cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+category"} factorization category
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p The following constructions were used by Baues and Wirsching and we will more or less adapt their terminology and notation.
          %p
            Given a small category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %strong category of factorizations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FI
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FI
            as follows. The objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FI
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FI
            are the morphisms of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:i\to j
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y : k\to l
            is a commutative square of the form (note the direction of arrows!)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi i
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟵
                            %mi v
                        %mtd
                          %mi k
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %msup
                            %mi y
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi j
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  i &amp; \stackrel{v}\longleftarrow &amp; k\\
                  {x}^{\mathllap{}}\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow{y}^{\mathrlap{}}\\
                  j &amp;\stackrel{u}\longrightarrow &amp; l
                  }
          %p
            In other words, it is a pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u,v)
            which factorizes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = u\circ x \circ v
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi v
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u',v')\circ (u,v) := (u'\circ u, v\circ v')
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(f)
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_f
            and uses the abbreviation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_i = D_{id_i}
            for every object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_* = D(u,Id) : D_x\to D_{u x}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi v
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Id
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v^* = D(Id, v) : D_x\to D_{x v}
          %h2#natural_systems Natural systems
          %p
            A
            %strong natural system
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is a functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo :
                    %mi FI
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D : FI\to Ab
          %p The evident notion of a non-Abelian natural system does not behave that well. A lax version (introduced by Wells) handles that case.
          %p Simple examples of natural systems are provided from the consideration of the sequence of functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FI
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi x
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi I
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi j
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  FI \stackrel{(i\overset{x}\to j)\mapsto (i,j)}\longrightarrow
                  I^{op}\times I \stackrel{(i,j)\mapsto j}\longrightarrow I
          %p
            which implies that any functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\to Ab
            and, more generally, any functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{op}\times I\to Ab
            provides (after precomposing with the canonical functor from
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FI
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FI
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
          %p
            Natural systems on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            are the coefficients of a
            %strong Baues-Wirsching cohomology
            of small categories. Namely, the Baues-Wirsching cochain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*(I,D)
            has
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mi ⋯
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C^n(I,D) = \prod D_{x_1\cdots x_n}
          %p
            where the product is over all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi n
                    %mi …
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_n\stackrel{x_n}\longrightarrow \ldots \stackrel{x_1}\longrightarrow i_0
          %h2#references References
          %p
            (A more extensive list may be found at the entry on
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baues-Wirsching+cohomology"} Baues-Wirsching cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H.-J.+Baues"}> H.-J. Baues
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Algebraic+Homotopy"} Algebraic Homotopy
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 15
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H.-J.+Baues"}> H.-J. Baues
                , G. Wirsching,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cohomology of small categories
                %strong 38
                (1985), 187-211.
            %li
              %p
                T. Pirashvili,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the center and Baues-Wirsching cohomology
                %strong 16
                (2009) 1, 131-144
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C.+Wells"}> C. Wells
                ,
                %em Extension theories for categories (preliminary report)
                (1979), available here
              %p http://www.cwru.edu/artsci/math/wells/pub/pdf/catext.pdf).
